# Async and Event Hygiene

## No `Task.Run` — ever

WebGL has no threads (see the webgl no-threads rule). `Task.Run`, `ConfigureAwait(false)`, and thread-pool continuations either fail or silently run on the main thread. Use coroutines or `Awaitable`/main-thread `async` for time-sliced work.

## No `Task.Delay` — ever

`Task.Delay` uses a thread-pool timer that never fires on WebGL — awaited delays hang forever. Use `MainThreadDelay.WaitAsync` / `WaitMillisecondsAsync` or `Awaitable.WaitForSecondsAsync` with a `CancellationToken` (e.g. `destroyCancellationToken`).

## `async void` is banned (one exception)

`async void` swallows exceptions and can't be awaited or cancelled. The only allowed use is a thin adapter for a Unity message/UI callback that immediately awaits a `Task`-returning method and catches at the top.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — fire-and-forget, exceptions vanish
private async void LoadProfile() { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — Task-returning core, adapter only at the event boundary
private async void OnLoadClicked()  // UI callback boundary
{
    try
    {
        await LoadProfileAsync();
    }
    catch (Exception e)
    {
        Debug.LogException(e);
    }
}

private async Task LoadProfileAsync() { ... }
```

## Async work must respect object lifetime

Play mode can exit, and objects can be destroyed, while an `await` is pending. After every `await` in a MonoBehaviour, guard before touching Unity state:

```csharp
await FetchAsync();
if (this == null)
{
    return;  // destroyed while awaiting — lifecycle guard, silent return is OK here
}
```

Prefer `CancellationToken` (e.g. `destroyCancellationToken`) over ad-hoc flags when available.

## Events: subscribe and unsubscribe symmetrically

Every `+=` / `AddListener` has a matching `-=` / `RemoveListener` in the mirrored lifecycle method: `Awake`↔`OnDestroy`, `OnEnable`↔`OnDisable`.

```csharp
private void OnEnable()
{
    GameEvents.RoundEnded += OnRoundEnded;
}

private void OnDisable()
{
    GameEvents.RoundEnded -= OnRoundEnded;
}
```

- **Static events** must also be cleared in the project's static-reset hub (see the unity domain-reload rule) — domain reload is disabled, so stale handlers survive play sessions.
- Subscribe with method groups, not lambdas — a lambda can't be unsubscribed and leaks the subscriber.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No `Task.Run`, `Task.Delay`, or thread-pool APIs
- [ ] No `async void` except top-level Unity/UI callback adapters with try/catch
- [ ] Every `await` in a MonoBehaviour followed by lifetime guard or cancellation token
- [ ] Every subscribe has a symmetric unsubscribe; no lambda subscriptions
- [ ] New static events registered in the static-reset hub
